\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{An edge derivative is half its variance}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the \vocabk{induced action}{fdip-005R}, with #{m>0} fixed,
##{\frac{\partial\cW}{\partial w_e}
 =\frac12\langle d_e,Gd_e\rangle
 =\frac12\mathbb E[(\phi_{h(e)}-\phi_{s(e)})^2],}
where #{\phi\sim N(0,G)} and the pairing is the counting inner
product. Each derivative is nonnegative. The counting-basis
matrices and the determinant-provider boundary are given in
\ref{fdip-00LP}.}

\proof{
\p{Choose an orthonormal basis of #{\mathbb R^V} and represent
the operators by their symmetric matrices in that basis. For any
such square matrix #{B}, multilinearity of the determinant gives
#{\det(\bbI+tB)=1+t\trace B+O(t^2)}: the linear terms select
one diagonal entry of #{tB}, and all higher terms use at least two
entries of #{tB}. Factoring an invertible #{Q} yields
##{\det(Q+tA)=\det Q\det(\bbI+tQ^{-1}A).}
When #{Q>0} and #{A} is symmetric, the perturbed matrix is positive
for sufficiently small #{|t|}. Differentiating its real logarithm
therefore gives
##{\left.\frac{\extd}{\extd t}\log\det(Q+tA)\right|_{t=0}
 =\trace(Q^{-1}A).}
Set #{A} equal to the matrix of #{d_e\otimes d_e^\flat}. Cyclicity
of trace gives
#{\trace(GA)=\langle d_e,Gd_e\rangle}. Finally the
[covariance identity](fdip-000B) says
#{\mathbb E[\langle d_e,\phi\rangle^2]=\langle d_e,Gd_e\rangle\ge0}.
Since #{\langle d_e,\phi\rangle=\phi_{h(e)}-\phi_{s(e)}}, this is the
displayed edge variance.}
}

\p{The matrix differential is the log-determinant gradient in
\citet{Appendix A.4.1, pp. 641-642}{boyd2004convex}.
The finite-matrix provider boundary, including the unit-determinant
hypothesis, is \ref{fdip-00LP}.}
